Dear All3DP Editors,

Solidon3D 0.2.2 has been released, and there is a simple five-minute test that
fits your practical CAD and STL coverage: import a useful model with the wrong
mounting hole, click the hole, and enter a new diameter without finding the
source CAD file or rebuilding the part.

Solidon preserves the original position and axis, including angled holes, and
stores the change as a reversible operation. The reader can then add a
standards-based heat-set insert, screw seat or bearing seat and export the
result to the slicer.

The reader-facing additions since the first 0.1 demo now include:

* constrained sketching and pull-to-solid or pull-to-pocket previews directly
  in the 3D view;
* reusable parametric components built from history steps without code;
* matching printable screws and nuts plus standards-based bearing seats;
* individual filament-spool settings carried to the correct 3MF material slot;
* Windows, macOS and Linux packages, with no account or cloud project store.

Solidon targets the gap between STL repair and learning a full CAD system. The
complete demo is free until 30 October 2026; version 1.0 follows as a one-time
purchase rather than a subscription.

Demo and screenshots: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Detailed changes: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

I can provide the released 0.2.2 build, the deliberately flawed STL, exact
before-and-after measurements and publication-ready images. There are no
conditions on the verdict.

